<div class="content-col"><h1 class="page-title">eDistrict Helpline: Where to Contact When Your Certificate Is Stuck — and in What Order</h1><p class="note">Note: This is an independent information guide — it is not the official portal (edistrict.up.gov.in).</p>
<p>Weeks have passed since you applied, the status has not moved, and you cannot figure out who to talk to — most people searching “edistrict email id” and “helpline number” are at exactly this point. The internet is full of numbers, but the plain truth is that a wrong number only wastes your time. This article lists only the contacts that belong to the official system and are verified: the eDistrict helpdesk phone and email, the Chief Minister Helpline 1076, a complaint on IGRS, and finally the appeal under the Janhit Guarantee Act. It also gives the right order — because most cases are resolved at the very first step; people simply do not know the order.</p>
<figure class="blog-featured"><img src="/images/blog/edistrict-helpline-contact-featured.webp" alt="Who to contact when a certificate is stuck — in the right order (example illustration)" /><figcaption>Example illustration — edistrictupseva.com</figcaption></figure>
<section class="box"><h2>First of all — check the status, read the remark</h2><p>Before calling anyone, make your own case clear. Check the application status with the application number (SRN) — the method is in our <a href="/en/status-verification/">Status &amp; Verification guide</a>. The <strong>remark</strong> shown in the status is what tells you who holds the file and why it is stuck — a missing document, pending verification, or waiting with an officer. This same information is needed in every contact that follows; without the SRN and the remark, even the helpdesk cannot help you properly. If your problem is login or password, first see the <a href="/en/blog/esathi-login-forgot-password-otp/">eSathi login problems</a> article — that problem is often solved at home itself.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Official contacts — at a glance</h2><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Contact</th><th>For what</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>eDistrict Helpdesk (CeG, UP)</td><td>Phone: <strong>0522-2304706</strong><br/>Email: <strong>ceghelpdesk@gmail.com</strong></td><td>Technical and process difficulties about application status, login/password, payment and certificates</td></tr><tr><td>Chief Minister Helpline</td><td><strong>1076</strong> (toll-free)</td><td>A complaint about delay or negligence in any government service — you receive a complaint number</td></tr><tr><td>IGRS / Jansunwai portal</td><td>Online complaint portal</td><td>For registering a written complaint and tracking its status online</td></tr><tr><td>Official portal</td><td><strong>edistrict.up.gov.in</strong> (citizen login: eSathi)</td><td>For applying, checking status and downloading certificates</td></tr><tr><td>This website (guide corrections only)</td><td>edistrictupseva@gmail.com</td><td>An error, outdated information or a topic suggestion in our guides — we cannot help with applications</td></tr></tbody></table><figure class="blog-featured"><img src="/images/blog/helpline-route-flow.svg" alt="The right contact order — check status, call the helpdesk, complain on CM Helpline 1076 or IGRS, then the Janhit appeal (diagram)" /><figcaption>The complaint route — four steps, in this order</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Step 1 — Contact the helpdesk (phone / email)</h2><p>The first official door is the helpdesk of the Centre for e-Governance (CeG), Uttar Pradesh — phone <strong>0522-2304706</strong> and email <strong>ceghelpdesk@gmail.com</strong>. This is the same contact already given as the official support on our <a href="/en/contact-us/">Contact page</a>.</p><p><strong>On the phone:</strong> keep the SRN number, service name, district and the status remark written down beforehand. At the end of the conversation, if a complaint/reference number is given, note it without fail.</p><p><strong>By email:</strong> put the service and SRN in the subject — for example, “Income Certificate — SRN … — status pending”. In the message, write the application date, district-tehsil, the status remark and your problem clearly in two or three lines. You may attach the receipt or a status screenshot if relevant — but <strong>never write your password or OTP in an email</strong>.</p><div class="note">Many “helpline numbers” circulating on the internet are unverified — this article deliberately keeps only the numbers/email that match the official support system. Do not give your personal information to an unknown number.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Step 2 — Complain: CM Helpline 1076 and IGRS</h2><p>If the helpdesk does not resolve the matter, or the delay keeps growing, the next step is a <strong>complaint</strong>. The Chief Minister Helpline <strong>1076</strong> is toll-free — a delay in any government service can be reported there, and you receive a complaint number, which makes follow-up easier. For a written, on-record complaint, register online on the <strong>IGRS (Jansunwai) portal</strong> — the complaint’s status can also be tracked online there.</p><p>Understand the difference between a complaint and an appeal: a complaint raises the matter and demands an answer, but it is not a legal appeal. If the application has been <strong>rejected</strong>, or the prescribed time limit has passed, the real strength lies in the Janhit Guarantee Act appeal — that is the next step. If you need nearby help, your district’s CSC / Jan Seva Kendra can also guide you — the precautions for centres are in the <a href="/en/csc-guide/">CSC guide</a>; there too, do not give your OTP/password to anyone.</p><h3>If you applied through a CSC, start at that centre</h3><p>If the application was filed at a Jan Seva Kendra, first go back to the same centre with your SRN and receipt and ask the operator to show the status remark on screen and read it — often the gap is found right there, such as an unclear document or an incomplete detail. If the gap is on your side, the correction process becomes clear there itself; if the matter is pending with an officer, take the centre’s information and use the helpdesk and complaint steps above. Do not share your login details or OTP at the centre; a status check works with the SRN alone.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Step 3 — The Janhit Guarantee appeal (the legal route)</h2><p>Under the Uttar Pradesh Janhit Guarantee Act, 2011, if a service is not delivered in the fixed time or an application is rejected, the legal route of a first appeal (Tehsildar to SDM level) and then a second appeal (DM level) opens against the designated officer’s decision. The full process, time limits and the penalty rule are given step by step in our <a href="/en/blog/janhit-guarantee-appeal-delayed-certificate/">Janhit Guarantee appeal article</a>. Keep this order — first status and helpdesk, then complaint, and finally the appeal; most cases are resolved at the first or second step itself.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Before you contact — keep this ready</h2><ul class="checklist"><li>The application number (SRN) and the date the application was submitted.</li><li>The service name and district/tehsil — that is where the file sits.</li><li>The latest remark shown in the status — a screenshot if possible.</li><li>The payment receipt or a copy of the application, if the problem is about payment/submission.</li><li>Note down the reference number of every call/complaint — the next conversation continues from there.</li><li>If the work is time-bound (admission, recruitment, scholarship), state its last date clearly when you make contact.</li></ul></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Safety — never do this</h2><ul class="checklist"><li>Do not give your OTP, password or Aadhaar number to anyone — even a genuine helpdesk never asks for these.</li><li>Beware of unknown agents and numbers promising an “instant certificate” — applications happen only on the official portal or at an authorised centre.</li><li>Do not post your SRN, certificate number or photos of your documents in public groups.</li><li>Always make contact through the channels given in this article and on the official portal — not every number shown in search results is a government number.</li></ul></section>
